  "text": "To solve the problem, we need to find the values of \\(p\\), \\(q\\), \\(r\\), and \\(s\\) such that \\(\\sin^2 C + \\sin^2 A + 2 \\cos C \\cos A \\sin B = \\frac{p+q\\sqrt{r}}{s}\\). We will use the given equations:\n\n1. \\(\\sin^2 A + \\sin^2 B + 2 \\cos A \\cos B \\sin C = \\frac{14}{9}\\)\n2. \\(\\sin^2 B + \\sin^2 C + 2 \\cos B \\cos C \\sin A = \\frac{65}{36}\\)\n\nWe start by introducing new variables to simplify the equations:\nLet \\(x = \\sin^2 A\\), \\(y = \\sin^2 B\\), and \\(z = \\sin^2 C\\). Then the equations become:\n1. \\(x + y + 2 \\cos A \\cos B \\sin C = \\frac{14}{9}\\)\n2. \\(y + z + 2 \\cos B \\cos C \\sin A = \\frac{65}{36}\\)\n\nSince \\(\\cos A \\cos B \\sin C\\) and \\(\\cos B \\cos C \\sin A\\) can be expressed in terms of the angles of the triangle, we use the fact that \\(A + B + C = \\pi\\) and the identities for the angles of a triangle. We will use the identity \\(\\cos A = \\cos (\\pi - B - C) = -\\cos (B + C)\\) and \\(\\cos B = \\cos (\\pi - A - C) = -\\cos (A + C)\\).\n\nHowever, a simpler approach is to use the fact that \\(\\cos A \\cos B \\sin C = \\frac{1}{2} [\\cos (A+B+C) - \\cos (A+B-C)] = \\frac{1}{2} [\\cos \\pi - \\cos (A+B-C)] = \\frac{1}{2} [-1 - \\cos (A+B-C)] = -\\frac{1}{2} - \\cos (A+B-C)\\) and similarly for \\(\\cos B \\cos C \\sin A\\).\n\nThis simplifies our equations to:\n1. \\(x + y + 2 (-\\frac{1}{2} - \\cos (A+B-C)) = \\frac{14}{9}\\)\n2. \\(y + z + 2 (-\\frac{1}{2} - \\cos (B+C-A)) = \\frac{65}{36}\\)\n\nSimplifying further:\n1. \\(x + y - 1 - 2 \\cos (A+B-C) = \\frac{14}{9}\\)\n2. \\(y + z - 1 - 2 \\cos (B+C-A) = \\frac{65}{36}\\)\n\nThis gives us:\n1. \\(x + y - 2 \\cos (A+B-C) = \\frac{23}{9}\\)\n2. \\(y + z - 2 \\cos (B+C-A) = \\frac{89}{36}\\)\n\nSince \\(A + B + C = \\pi\\), we have \\(A + B - C = \\pi - 2C\\) and similarly for the other terms. This simplifies to:\n1. \\(x + y - 2 \\cos (\\pi - 2C) = \\frac{23}{9}\\)\n2. \\(y + z - 2 \\cos (\\pi - 2A) = \\frac{89}{36}\\)\n\nUsing the identity \\(\\cos (\\pi - \\theta) = -\\cos \\theta\\), we get:\n1. \\(x + y + 2 \\cos (2C) = \\frac{23}{9}\\)\n2. \\(y + z + 2 \\cos (2A) = \\frac{89}{36}\\)\n\nWe now need to find \\(\\sin^2 C + \\sin^2 A + 2 \\cos C \\cos A \\sin B\\). Using the same identity, we get:\n\\(\\sin^2 C + \\sin^2 A + 2 \\cos C \\cos A \\sin B = \\frac{23}{9} + \\frac{89}{36} = \\frac{89}{36} + \\frac{89}{36} = \\frac{178}{36} = \\frac{89}{18}\\).\n\nThus, we have \\(p = 89\\), \\(q = 0\\), \\(r = 18\\), and \\(s = 18\\). Therefore, \\(p + q + r + s = 89 + 0 + 18 + 18 = 125\\).\n\nThe final answer is \\(\\boxed{125}\\).",
